  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-24T07:45:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "807334cb-96a5-4491-91d4-d4466b4a7a43",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 240745\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1245 AM PDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north winds today with lingering shower and storm chances in \n  northwestern Arizona.\n\n* Quiet and seasonable conditions through at least Wednesday, with\n  signs of a more active pattern returning late in the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Halloween.\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a trough overhead with a vort max over \nsouthern CA in the process of pinching off into a closed low. Behind \nthe shortwave, gusty north winds are currently pushing through the \nsouthern Great Basin and into the northern Mojave Desert. These will \ncontinue advance southward, encompassing most of the CWA by sunrise. \nStrongest winds anticipated in Death Valley and the southern Owens \nValley where gusts of 40-50 mph are possible through the morning \nhours. Elsewhere, expecting gusts generally 20-35 mph, weakening \nduring the 2nd half of the day. The formation of the closed low will \ncause the system to slowly exit the region, lingering over \nnorthwestern AZ this afternoon. The cold air aloft, residual \nmoisture, and daytime heating should drive isolated to scattered \nshowers and maybe even a few storms over eastern Mohave County (20-\n40%), with lower chances (10-20%) over southeastern NV and the \nColorado River Valley. Can't completely rule out an isolated \ninstance of flash flooding in eastern Mohave County, but odds are 5% \nat best.\n\nOur low finally exits on Sunday, leaving us in a zonal pattern for a \nfew days before transitioning to southwesterly flow later in the \nweek. From Sunday - Thursday, the forecast calls for seasonable \ntemperatures, light breezes, and dry conditions. Late in the week, \nensemble guidance shows an energized subtropical jet trying to push \nmoisture into the southwestern US. Cluster analysis shows several \nvariations of offshore troughing during this time, so there remains \na fair amount of uncertainty, but there appears to be a good signal \nfor the existence of an offshore trough and southwesterly flow over \nus. Should this come to fruition, we should see an increase in \nsouthwest winds and low to moderate (10-50%) precipitation chances \naround Halloween weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nnorthwest will persist a few more hours. A push of stronger northwest \nwinds are expected tonight, likely after 09Z. Gusts up to 25KT are \nexpected with these stronger winds with the push of winds, \nespecially at onset. Gusty northwest will continue through Saturday \nmorning into the afternoon, then will slowly diminishing 21Z. Gusts \nshould end by 00Z. Northwest will continue into Saturday evening but \nwill remain at 10-15KT for the first part of the night, then lighter \nwinds will arrive by 06Z. VFR mostly clear skies will persist \nthrough the TAF period.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Expect an increase in \nwinds overnight at most locations as a cold front pushes through the \nregion. Gusts 25-35KT are expected behind the front. Gusty north to \nnorthwest winds last into the first half of the day Saturday, then \nlikely begin diminishing late afternoon and evening. Mostly clear \nskies last through the TAF period. Other a few isolated showers in \nMohave County Saturday night, it will be dry across the region.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
